From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add the measured RGMII delays to gmac0

Patch `arm64-dts-a733-add-gmac0-ethernet` set phy-mode = "rgmii-id" on the assumption that the Maxio driver
adds the whole RGMII clock delay at the PHY, and it flagged that choice as the
one item still to validate at a real 1000M link. Validated now, on hardware, and
the assumption does not hold.

With the MAC delay fields of GMAC_CFG left at zero the link trains normally --
"Link is Up - 1Gbps/Full - flow control rx/tx" -- but not a single frame
survives the data path: udhcpc gets no lease ("broadcasting discover / no
lease") and ping loses 100 %. Link training only exercises autonegotiation, so
"link up" says nothing about RGMII timing.

Sweeping the MAC TX delay on the running board, RX held at the vendor's 10
(8 GiB board, mainline 6.18.36, 2026-07-21, gateway on a gigabit switch):

	GMAC_CFG    TX     result
	0x00000146   0     100 % packet loss
	0x00001146   4     100 % packet loss
	0x00001d46   7       0 % packet loss
	0x00011146  12       0 % packet loss   (the vendor value)

700 ps is therefore the smallest working setting, and it matters that it works:
the mainline glue programs ETXDC through SYSCON_ETXDC_MASK = GENMASK(12, 10),
three bits, so it cannot express the vendor's 12 at all -- that would need the
ETXDC_H bits 17:16, which the driver does not model. Had only 12 worked this
would have been a driver patch instead of a DT one.

RX keeps the vendor's 1000 ps; it was not swept independently, so it is carried
over rather than claimed as minimal.

Verified under load at TX 700 / RX 1000: 40/40 pings at 0 % loss, and a 41 MB
HTTP transfer whose sha256 matched the source byte for byte.

phy-mode stays "rgmii-id", which is the mode the measurement was taken in, and
the PHY side was read back rather than assumed. Patch `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver`'s config_init ORs
TX_DELAY (bits 6,7) and RX_DELAY (bits 4,5) into page 0xd96 register 0 for
PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII_ID, so that register should read 0x11f0 once the driver
has run. A clause-22 read driven straight through MAC_MDIO_Address/Data on the
running board returns 0x00001140: bits 6, 8 and 12 set, bit 7 clear and both RX
bits clear. The PHY-side delay write therefore does not land, which is why the
MAC has to carry the whole delay -- and it is a bug in `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` in its own right,
tracked separately. Should that write ever start working, these two values must
be re-measured, not kept.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1392,6 +1392,22 @@
 			pinctrl-names = "default";
 			pinctrl-0 = <&rgmii0_pins>;
 			phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
+			/*
+			 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-21 (8 GiB board, 1000M
+			 * link to the switch): the PHY-side delay implied by
+			 * "rgmii-id" does NOT carry the link on its own. With
+			 * GMAC_CFG delays at zero the link trains at 1 Gbps but
+			 * no frame survives -- DHCP gets no lease and every ping
+			 * is lost. Sweeping the MAC TX delay at RX = 10 gave
+			 * 100 % loss at TX 0 and TX 4, and 0 % loss at TX 7 and
+			 * TX 12 (the vendor value). 700 ps is the lowest setting
+			 * that works and stays inside the 3-bit ETXDC field the
+			 * mainline glue can program; RX follows the vendor at
+			 * 1000 ps. Verified under load: 40/40 pings and a 41 MB
+			 * HTTP transfer that matched sha256 byte for byte.
+			 */
+			tx-internal-delay-ps = <700>;
+			rx-internal-delay-ps = <1000>;
 			phy-handle = <&ext_rgmii_phy>;
 			phy-supply = <&reg_swout1>;
 			status = "okay";
